<Docs slug="agents">
  <h1>Agents</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Agents are first-class users of Ouijit. This page is the contract an agent can rely on inside an Ouijit
    terminal, and the workflows it should use to drive the app.
  </p>

  <h2>What an agent receives</h2>
  <p>
    Every <a href="/docs/harnesses/">supported harness</a> gets the <code>ouijit</code> CLI reference appended to
    its system prompt automatically, from <code>~/.config/Ouijit/ouijit-cli-reference.md</code>. It covers the
    commands in these docs plus behavioral notes: JSON output on stdout, errors as
    <code>{`{"error": "..."}`}</code> on stderr with a non-zero exit, and the flags for headless status changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a sandboxed terminal the reference opens with a short section of its own: that the terminal is sandboxed,
    what Ouijit lets it write, the project's CLI access level, and to ask with <code>ouijit ask run</code> when the
    sandbox blocks a command rather than work around it. What else the sandbox allows is up to its profile or
    launcher, so the section doesn't list it.
  </p>

  <h2>Environment</h2>
  <p>Every Ouijit terminal has these set. An agent can read them directly:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_API_URL</code></td><td>Base URL of the local REST API the CLI talks to. Unset outside Ouijit terminals, so scripts can detect the environment.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_API_TOKEN</code></td><td>Bearer token scoped to this terminal session.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_PTY_ID</code></td><td>This terminal's id. <code>ouijit task current</code> and the panel commands resolve through it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><a href="/docs/hooks/#hook-environment">Hooks</a> additionally receive <code>OUIJIT_TASK_*</code> context.</p>

  <h2>Drive the board</h2>
  <p>An agent working a task should resolve its own task first, then advance it when the work is committed:</p>
  <pre class="glass-bevel"><code>TASK=$(ouijit task current | jq -r .taskNumber)
ouijit task set-status "$TASK" in_review --skip-hook</code></pre>
  <p>
    By default a status change whose hook would ask first waits behind a <a href="/docs/cli/#point">pointer</a>
    at the task, and the dialog opens when the user follows it. To run without waiting, pass
    <code>--run-hook</code> (run the configured hook immediately), <code>--skip-hook</code> (no hook), or
    <code>--hook-command "&lt;cmd&gt;"</code> (a one-off command). The same flags apply to
    <code>task start</code> and <code>task create-and-start</code>. The hook runs where its
    <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandbox</a> setting says, or the project default, unless
    <code>--sandbox host|nono|custom</code> says otherwise. <code>nono</code> is Ouijit's sandbox.
  </p>

  <h2>Open panels beside yourself</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can put its plan or its running app next to its own terminal. Both commands default to the current
    terminal via <code>OUIJIT_PTY_ID</code>:
  </p>
  <pre class="glass-bevel"><code>ouijit markdown add ./plan.md
ouijit preview add http://localhost:3000</code></pre>

  <h2>Review without posting</h2>
  <p>
    For anything that belongs to a pull request review, agents default to <code>ouijit pr</code> over
    <code>gh</code>. <code>gh</code> posts to GitHub the moment it runs, under the user's own account;
    <code>ouijit pr draft</code> stages comments locally and the user sends the review themselves. Reading with
    <code>gh pr diff</code> is always fine, and a <code>gh</code> write the user asks for by name &mdash;
    <code>gh pr create</code>, say &mdash; still goes through, as them.
  </p>
  <pre class="glass-bevel"><code>PR=$(ouijit task current | jq .githubPrNumber)
gh pr diff $PR                                # read it
ouijit pr draft add $PR --file src/api.ts --line 88 \
  --origin claude --body "this can throw when the token is missing"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Pass <code>--origin</code> with your name so the user can tell agent comments apart before sending, and
    <code>--body -</code> to read multi-line bodies from stdin. Anchor each comment to a line that appears as an
    added line in the diff, by its new-file line number: GitHub rejects the whole review at submit time if any
    comment points outside the diff.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <a href="/docs/pull-requests/">Linear</a> comment works the same way, and for the same reason. Nothing an
    agent runs moves an issue &mdash; that is a control on the issue, pressed by someone looking at it.
  </p>
  <pre class="glass-bevel"><code>ISSUE=$(ouijit task current | jq -r .linearIssueIdentifier)
ouijit linear view $ISSUE                     # read it
ouijit linear draft add $ISSUE --origin claude \
  --body "the retry loop drops the last error"</code></pre>

  <h2>Inside a sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    Inside a <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandbox</a>, <code>ouijit</code> can do what the project's
    <a href="/docs/sandbox/#cli">Ouijit CLI access</a> allows: by default read its own task, stage comments,
    point and label its own terminal; also edit and move its task; or everything. At any level it can
    <a href="/docs/cli/#ask">ask you to run a command</a> outside the sandbox, which runs only when you press Run.
    A hook that would run on the host waits for you even with <code>--run-hook</code>. Pull request drafts written from a sandbox are stamped with a <code>sandbox</code> origin
    the caller cannot forge. Under the Custom sandbox, whether <code>ouijit</code> is reachable at all is up to
    the launcher.
  </p>

  <h2>Machine-readable docs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Markdown pages</strong>. Every docs page is served as plain Markdown at <code>https://ouijit.com/docs/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>, for example <a href="/docs/cli.md">/docs/cli.md</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Full docs in one file</strong>. <a href="https://ouijit.com/llms-full.txt">ouijit.com/llms-full.txt</a> concatenates every page.</li>
    <li><strong>Index with summaries</strong>. <a href="https://ouijit.com/llms.txt">ouijit.com/llms.txt</a> lists each page with a one-line summary.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
